{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Arial;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\lang1033\f0\fs24\b\fs26 MorphoAqua Stage-by-Stage Summary\b0\par\par\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs20\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage\cell Objective\cell Method\cell Result\cell Interpretation\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 1A\cell Establish the physical hover baseline and determine the thrust required to balance gravity.\cell Use a simplified rotor thrust model and calculate the total thrust, weight, and per-rotor thrust for a 1.5 kg robot.\cell The model estimates a total hover thrust of 14.715 N, or 3.679 N per rotor, corresponding to about 5791.91 RPM per rotor. Final position and velocity are approximately zero, representing a stable hover condition.\cell This is the foundation of the project. It confirms the robot can maintain equilibrium under ideal conditions and sets the operating point for all later control development.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 1B-1C\cell Add feedback control to move the robot from the ground to a target altitude and return to the ground.\cell Implement closed-loop altitude control using error between desired and actual vertical position; adjust rotor thrust to reduce that error.\cell The simulation demonstrates controlled ascent and descent with stabilizing correction, showing that the robot can perform active vertical motion rather than only static hover.\cell This stage transitions the project from equilibrium modeling to dynamic control, proving that the robot can carry out commanded vertical motion in a feedback loop.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 2A-2B\cell Extend the controller to full 3D motion and waypoint tracking.\cell Model the system in x, y, and z motion and command trajectory or waypoint-following tasks with position error feedback.\cell The generated 3D trajectory and tracking results show successful navigation through space, with converging position errors and stable control response.\cell This proves that the robot is not limited to vertical flight; it can move in 3D and follow prescribed paths, which is essential for real deployment.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 3A-3B\cell Investigate how morphing changes the robot\'s dynamics and whether the control system remains stable during shape changes.\cell Modify the system configuration during simulation and observe controller behavior under altered mass distribution, inertia, and actuation properties.\cell The morphing-response plots show that the control system can maintain tracking and stabilize the vehicle despite structural reconfiguration.\cell This validates the main concept of a morphing aerial-aquatic robot: the platform can adapt its shape without destabilizing the controller.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 4A-4B\cell Evaluate performance in water-like or aquatic conditions to study the aerial-aquatic transition.\cell Simulate vehicle behavior using dynamics consistent with denser fluid environments and analyze position tracking and control response.\cell Position-tracking and water-response plots indicate sustained control performance even under water-affected dynamics.\cell This demonstrates that the platform is designed for operation across air and water, not just in a single environmental regime.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 5B1\cell Study how morphology and inertia changes affect system robustness.\cell Run simulations with parameter variation and inspect how inertia and shape affect response and tracking.\cell The morphology/inertia plots show measurable changes in response as characteristic parameters shift.\cell This stage highlights that performance depends on physical configuration and that controller robustness must account for changing inertial properties.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 5B2\cell Assess directional disturbances and impact sensitivity.\cell Introduce directional perturbations and measure controller response to off-axis forcing and impact-like disturbances.\cell The direction-impact-response plots show the system\'s transient reaction and recovery under perturbation.\cell This confirms that the robot is being evaluated against realistic external disturbances, not just idealized reference tracking.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 5B3\cell Improve estimation and state awareness by evaluating sensor estimation and position-estimation tracking.\cell Combine dynamic prediction with estimation of position or sensor states and compare estimated vs. true states.\cell The results show the system can estimate motion and sensor states with a reasonable degree of fidelity.\cell This is a major progression toward realistic operation because a real robot must rely on imperfect measurements and estimation, not exact state knowledge.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 5B4\cell Add adaptive predictive control to improve robustness to changing conditions.\cell Use adaptive and predictive control logic to adjust responses to uncertainty and evaluate reachable and predictive trajectories.\cell The adaptive predictive response plots show improved trajectory quality and response under dynamic conditions.\cell This stage demonstrates the move from basic tracking to a more advanced uncertainty-aware control strategy designed for real-world variability.\cell\row
\trowd\cellx1800\cellx4200\cellx7000\cellx9800\cellx13800\intbl Stage 5C\cell Perform final robustness validation under nominal and perturbed conditions using quantitative summaries.\cell Compare nominal and current results, analyze disturbance reactions, and use Monte Carlo- or summary-based validation to assess system performance.\cell The final outputs include nominal/current comparisons, disturbance-response plots, Monte Carlo data, and robustness summaries indicating performance across conditions.\cell This is the strongest validation stage of the project, showing that the system remains useful not only in ideal simulations but also under uncertainty, variation, and disturbances.\cell\row
